    54	efficient `O(m n^2)` algorithm (`O(n^3)` here). At shipping seed 314159 it used
    55	83,678 counted bit operations and about 0.00096 seconds. Across the eight G6
    56	seeds the largest count was 119,459; all eight were solved, as Track B expects.
    57	The compact route notices that every distinct row is `J+I`: each row contains
    58	all coordinates except one. One global parity followed by coordinatewise XORs
    59	recovers the key in 288 counted exact operations. The benchmark tests whether a
    60	no-tool solver finds that collapse before attempting mechanical elimination.
    61	
    62	## Worked demo (seed 9)
    63	
    64	This is the complete `demo` rendering. It is genuinely hand-solvable: align the
    65	four complement rows and solve the four XOR equations.

